1
00:00:11,219 --> 00:00:12,595
Now, this is beautiful, no?

2
00:00:13,054 --> 00:00:16,057
Camille Pissarro,
dean of the impressionists.

3
00:00:16,766 --> 00:00:21,854
Notice how he's abandoned precision
in favor of expression, how alive it all is.

4
00:00:29,195 --> 00:00:30,404
What about this?

5
00:00:30,696 --> 00:00:34,408
Well, that, I'm afraid, is not for sale.
It's entirely too valuable.

6
00:00:34,617 --> 00:00:35,660
Right, Silvia?

7
00:00:37,245 --> 00:00:38,371
Watch out!

8
00:00:46,087 --> 00:00:47,296
Go, go!

9
00:00:48,965 --> 00:00:51,008
- Back! Up against the desk!
- What are you doing?!

10
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:52,927
- I said move!
- 60 seconds!

11
00:00:53,052 --> 00:00:54,929
- Pick it up! Let's go!
- Let's go!

12
00:00:55,096 --> 00:00:57,098
- Stay back, Silvia.
- Come on! Clock is running!

13
00:00:57,223 --> 00:00:59,684
Come on! Move!
Go, go, go! Move!

14
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:00,851
Take it!

15
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:03,813
Go! Come on!
Pick up your feet!

16
00:01:04,146 --> 00:01:05,356
No, no, no,
don't you touch that!

17
00:01:05,481 --> 00:01:07,525
- Don't be stupid.
- Time is running!

18
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:09,068
I'm telling you,
that's worth nothing to you.

19
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:11,237
- Take it.
- John, no. John, don't!

20
00:01:16,826 --> 00:01:19,412
- We're good!
- Let's go! Let's go! Let's go!

21
00:01:22,164 --> 00:01:23,666
Somebody help me!

22
00:01:23,916 --> 00:01:25,126
Go, go, go!

23
00:01:52,685 --> 00:01:53,936
What's all this?

24
00:01:54,979 --> 00:01:56,855
Oh, well,
that's the art squad.

25
00:01:57,314 --> 00:01:59,233
They deal with stolen art
and stuff like that.

26
00:01:59,525 --> 00:02:01,944
Yeah, their offices on the sixth
floor are being remodeled

27
00:02:02,069 --> 00:02:04,738
{\an8}so I told them to set up in the conference
room for the time being.

28
00:02:09,577 --> 00:02:14,164
Mrs. Hennigan, why was your husband
trying to save this painting in particular?

29
00:02:14,498 --> 00:02:15,708
That's me.

30
00:02:16,458 --> 00:02:19,461
{\an8}John painted it years ago,
when we first met.

31
00:02:20,337 --> 00:02:22,298
{\an8}We fell in love
as he was painting it.

32
00:02:24,133 --> 00:02:27,761
{\an8}It's not worth any money,
but it was worth a lot to us.

33
00:02:30,514 --> 00:02:31,640
To me.

34
00:02:32,558 --> 00:02:34,435
We'll do our best
to get it back for you.

35
00:02:34,685 --> 00:02:36,228
This gang
that did this, uh...

36
00:02:36,562 --> 00:02:38,564
{\an8}We've been chasing them
for quite a while now.

37
00:02:39,106 --> 00:02:40,733
{\an8}They're really good
at what they do.

38
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:44,987
I can't believe he's gone.

39
00:02:45,404 --> 00:02:46,989
Well, he's not gone.

40
00:02:48,407 --> 00:02:52,202
{\an8}He's alive as long as you're alive
to remember him. He lives in you.

41
00:02:55,414 --> 00:02:58,542
{\an8}It's a cliché. I know.
But it's true.

42
00:02:59,918 --> 00:03:02,588
{\an8}I'm special agent Marcus Pike.
This is agent Searls.

43
00:03:02,713 --> 00:03:06,050
You're Patrick Jane, right?
Abbott's guy.

44
00:03:06,175 --> 00:03:09,845
- Well, he's my guy.
- Thanks for sharing your floor with us.

45
00:03:10,012 --> 00:03:11,513
You people need some help, huh?

46
00:03:12,181 --> 00:03:14,350
- What makes you say that?
- His shoulders.

47
00:03:15,225 --> 00:03:16,935
{\an8}- Hey, now, uh...
- Hey, now, I'm just saying

48
00:03:17,061 --> 00:03:18,562
{\an8}if you need some help,
I could help you.

49
00:03:18,687 --> 00:03:21,190
{\an8}- I mean, murder is my business.
- Sure.

50
00:03:23,067 --> 00:03:25,486
I've heard good things about you.
We'd be happy to have your input.

51
00:03:25,611 --> 00:03:26,904
These guys, they're...

52
00:03:27,404 --> 00:03:31,617
They're really good and our cases
don't usually lead to homicide.

53
00:03:32,368 --> 00:03:34,536
{\an8}But you are going to
catch them, aren't you?

54
00:03:37,498 --> 00:03:40,918
{\an8}I hate to sound immodest, ma'am,
but, uh, I rarely fail.

55
00:03:41,794 --> 00:03:42,836
Patrick.

56
00:03:43,754 --> 00:03:46,173
{\an8}- Sylvia.
- Welcome to the fishbowl.

57
00:03:46,674 --> 00:03:48,300
{\an8}You volunteered us
to handle the case?

58
00:03:48,425 --> 00:03:50,469
I decide what cases
we take next.

59
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:54,181
Well, I was just being neighborly
and the art squad seemed all for it.

60
00:03:54,306 --> 00:03:55,641
Well, of course they were.

61
00:03:55,766 --> 00:03:57,851
They've been chasing this group
for years, with no luck.

62
00:03:57,976 --> 00:03:59,770
And what do you know
about catching art thieves?

63
00:04:00,479 --> 00:04:01,605
Nothing at all.

64
00:04:02,606 --> 00:04:03,982
That's why it'll be fun.

65
00:04:05,234 --> 00:04:06,944
Okay. We'll take it.

66
00:04:07,444 --> 00:04:09,613
Get a hold of agent Pike
in the art squad.

67
00:04:09,738 --> 00:04:11,448
{\an8}Get up to speed
on this thing.

68
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:12,908
{\an8}Let's show 'em how it's done.

69
00:04:17,121 --> 00:04:20,541
{\an8}This is special agent Marcus Pike
from the art-theft squad.

70
00:04:20,666 --> 00:04:22,543
{\an8}He's being attached to our team
while we work the case.

71
00:04:22,668 --> 00:04:24,712
Uh, I've asked him
to get you guys up to speed.

72
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:27,923
{\an8}Art thieves used to be
about sneaking...

73
00:04:28,048 --> 00:04:29,633
Nighttime break-ins.

74
00:04:29,758 --> 00:04:32,678
{\an8}Not anymore...
the new generation prefers guns.

75
00:04:33,053 --> 00:04:37,349
{\an8}This guy is the leader.
He does the talking, runs the show.

76
00:04:37,474 --> 00:04:39,727
But until yesterday morning,
he's never killed anybody.

77
00:04:39,852 --> 00:04:43,939
{\an8}They've done about six jobs between
Dallas and Phoenix in the last two years

78
00:04:44,064 --> 00:04:47,317
{\an8}taken down about $100 million
worth in art and artifacts.

79
00:04:47,443 --> 00:04:48,944
We're in the wrong business.

80
00:04:49,236 --> 00:04:51,155
{\an8}Stolen art can take
a long time to sell

81
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:53,615
{\an8}so our guess is that they're
sitting on a lot of the art

82
00:04:53,741 --> 00:04:55,701
{\an8}including the paintings
from yesterday's heist.

83
00:04:55,868 --> 00:04:58,620
{\an8}If we have those paintings we can
nail them for John Hennigan's murder

84
00:04:58,746 --> 00:05:00,873
{\an8}so it's imperative
that we find this art stash.

85
00:05:01,039 --> 00:05:03,500
{\an8}They must have left us
something to work with.

86
00:05:03,625 --> 00:05:05,002
Well, they've only
slipped up once.

87
00:05:05,127 --> 00:05:08,213
{\an8}We found a glove a few blocks
from a gallery they robbed in phoenix.

88
00:05:08,338 --> 00:05:10,758
{\an8}We managed to get a partial
print, and we got a hit.

89
00:05:11,508 --> 00:05:14,678
{\an8}Aaron Pulaski... time for
armed robbery and battery.

90
00:05:14,803 --> 00:05:17,681
He's also the former middleweight
boxing champ at Folsom prison.

91
00:05:17,806 --> 00:05:19,391
And you can't charge a man
for owning a glove.

92
00:05:19,516 --> 00:05:23,145
Yeah, we brought him in for questioning
but he lawyered up quick and told us zip.

93
00:05:23,312 --> 00:05:25,105
- These guys are pros.
- If he's a known felon

94
00:05:25,230 --> 00:05:27,649
we should have his known associates
hangouts, stuff like that.

95
00:05:27,775 --> 00:05:28,984
We do, but nothing's popped.

96
00:05:29,109 --> 00:05:32,905
He doesn't seem to have a lot of friends.
He hangs out a lot at a bar on 6th street.

97
00:05:33,655 --> 00:05:36,700
- We could set a tail on him.
- Eh, could be waiting for weeks.

98
00:05:37,159 --> 00:05:39,912
My plan is a little more elaborate
but it's much more entertaining.

99
00:05:40,037 --> 00:05:42,122
- Of course it is.
- You already have a plan?

100
00:05:42,456 --> 00:05:46,126
Well, as you know, agent Lisbon,
the key to a good con

101
00:05:46,251 --> 00:05:48,837
is always making the mark
feel that he is in control.

102
00:05:48,962 --> 00:05:52,966
I mean, you can lead a man anywhere
as long as he thinks that he is driving.

103
00:05:53,091 --> 00:05:54,218
So, how do we do that?

104
00:05:54,343 --> 00:05:56,553
Well, first, we rope him in
with some flash

105
00:05:56,678 --> 00:06:00,140
then we steer them towards
a couple of good inside men a face.

106
00:06:00,265 --> 00:06:03,393
We're gonna need some art for the gaff
and then a good false blow-off.

107
00:06:04,978 --> 00:06:07,773
- I don't know what any of that means.
- You'll get used to it.

108
00:06:08,774 --> 00:06:11,401
- But if you want art, I got art.
- Good.

109
00:06:13,779 --> 00:06:16,782
I always feel a little bit
like Aladdin right here.

110
00:06:24,414 --> 00:06:26,792
Okay. Now we're having fun.

111
00:06:28,252 --> 00:06:29,837
Where does all this stuff
come from?

112
00:06:29,962 --> 00:06:31,797
- It's stolen.
- What do you mean?

113
00:06:32,464 --> 00:06:34,341
Sometimes we get
just part of a haul

114
00:06:34,591 --> 00:06:36,426
and we sit on the art so the
crooks don't know have a lead

115
00:06:36,552 --> 00:06:38,804
and we keep it here while
we investigate the rest of it.

116
00:06:39,346 --> 00:06:40,806
And nobody knows
about it?

117
00:06:40,931 --> 00:06:44,351
Well, we keep a pretty tight
lid, our own secret museum.

118
00:06:44,726 --> 00:06:47,062
Impressive.
You bring dates here?

119
00:06:47,604 --> 00:06:51,567
No. Just... Once or twice.

120
00:06:52,442 --> 00:06:53,861
Fantastic. Gorgeous.

121
00:06:54,236 --> 00:06:56,238
I love you, agent Pike.
Everything we need.

122
00:06:56,363 --> 00:06:58,866
I'm gonna make you a list.
We should be ready to roll in about a week.

123
00:06:59,283 --> 00:07:01,285
Well, I... I don't want to
second-guess you, Mr. Jane

124
00:07:01,410 --> 00:07:03,495
but if you want to use
these paintings as bait

125
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:05,455
the guy running this crew
knows a lot about art.

126
00:07:05,581 --> 00:07:09,918
- He'll know this stuff is stolen.
- Yes. That's the point.

127
00:07:22,264 --> 00:07:25,350
- Good morning.
- Good morning.

128
00:07:25,475 --> 00:07:27,603
So, what's the plan
to catch these art thieves?

129
00:07:28,061 --> 00:07:30,898
Well, I am just as curious
as you are.

130
00:07:31,481 --> 00:07:34,818
Jane has been playing this real close
to his chest until he figures it out.

131
00:07:35,068 --> 00:07:37,696
He said he'll be here
to brief us any moment.

132
00:07:37,821 --> 00:07:38,947
You sure about that?

133
00:07:42,951 --> 00:07:44,036
Morning.

134
00:07:44,912 --> 00:07:46,163
Where's Jane?

135
00:07:46,288 --> 00:07:48,415
- Still at the party house.
- The party house?

136
00:07:48,707 --> 00:07:51,501
- Wait till you see it.
- Uh, w... this is his idea.

137
00:07:51,627 --> 00:07:54,922
How are we supposed to know what to do
if he's not here to tell us?

138
00:07:55,047 --> 00:07:56,214
He gave me notes.

139
00:07:58,967 --> 00:08:02,554
Okay, very well.
Please brief the team, agent Wylie.

140
00:08:03,221 --> 00:08:04,264
Okay.

141
00:08:07,225 --> 00:08:08,435
Here we go.

142
00:08:08,977 --> 00:08:11,813
We want the leader of the gang,
the one who shot John Hennigan.

143
00:08:11,939 --> 00:08:15,567
To find him, we need to gain
the trust of Pulaski.

144
00:08:17,361 --> 00:08:20,322
So, we know he likes to spend time
at the favorite bar.

145
00:08:20,739 --> 00:08:24,117
That's where we rope him. We need
to plant some bugs and cameras in the bar.

146
00:08:24,242 --> 00:08:26,370
- Why?
- So I can monitor it from here.

147
00:08:27,621 --> 00:08:28,914
You're our roper.

148
00:08:29,498 --> 00:08:31,416
Jane said you'd know about boxing.

149
00:08:32,751 --> 00:08:35,879
Well, I never told him,
but, yes, I do.

150
00:08:36,713 --> 00:08:39,049
So does Pulaski.
That's a good place to start.

151
00:08:39,174 --> 00:08:41,927
I've got some tips for you,
if you, uh...

152
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:46,765
But you... you probably know
what you're doing.

153
00:08:47,975 --> 00:08:51,144
You're the muscle. Jane said
that you could handle your own wardrobe.

154
00:08:52,437 --> 00:08:53,605
Yeah, I can do that.

155
00:08:55,190 --> 00:08:57,526
Jane said that the two of you
could work out the choreography.

156
00:08:57,651 --> 00:08:58,902
Oh, yeah. That's easy.

157
00:08:59,528 --> 00:09:02,864
Lisbon is playing the
inside man... woman...

158
00:09:02,990 --> 00:09:06,618
Inside woman, with Jane.
He wants you to wear this.

159
00:09:12,916 --> 00:09:15,794
- Yowza.
- It's a bit much, isn't it?

160
00:09:16,878 --> 00:09:18,880
This is for you.

161
00:09:21,591 --> 00:09:23,135
You're the face.

162
00:09:23,844 --> 00:09:27,597
Once Pulaski is at the party,
you lure him into the bedroom.

163
00:09:27,889 --> 00:09:29,599
Jane's got some ideas.

164
00:09:30,017 --> 00:09:32,978
Yeah. Thanks.
I think I've got it under control.

165
00:09:33,311 --> 00:09:34,980
Then Jane and Lisbon
Take over.

166
00:09:35,313 --> 00:09:36,815
And then what happens?

167
00:09:39,651 --> 00:09:42,029
"And then the fun really starts."

168
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:47,701
That's a direct quote.

169
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:15,020
Hey. Lone star.

170
00:10:16,688 --> 00:10:18,648
- We ready?
- Yeah. It just started.

171
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:29,785
God...

172
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:31,745
My man.

173
00:10:31,870 --> 00:10:33,413
Hey, let me get
some of those.

174
00:10:35,832 --> 00:10:37,250
That's a nice
opening gambit.

175
00:10:37,793 --> 00:10:39,753
- Well, how's that?
- You see what he did there?

176
00:10:40,337 --> 00:10:42,130
He asked him
for some napkins. So?

177
00:10:42,255 --> 00:10:43,673
Jane told me about this.

178
00:10:43,882 --> 00:10:46,259
You want someone to trust you,
you don't give them something.

179
00:10:46,384 --> 00:10:49,012
You get them to give you something,
even something small.

180
00:10:49,137 --> 00:10:51,765
It'll make them think they've invested
in you. It's all subconscious.

181
00:10:54,101 --> 00:10:56,645
Hey, my man, y'all showing
the Reynoso fight tomorrow?

182
00:10:57,604 --> 00:10:59,439
Oh, that's what's up. Okay.

183
00:11:00,107 --> 00:11:01,441
Whoo, baby.

184
00:11:02,526 --> 00:11:04,861
My bookie is gonna be
weeping about that.

185
00:11:04,986 --> 00:11:08,657
Reynoso is going down. You know,
that kid is a little bit too fast for him.

186
00:11:09,533 --> 00:11:11,034
Don't waste your money, bro.

187
00:11:11,743 --> 00:11:14,079
Kid's got speed, but he's got
no power and no chin.

188
00:11:15,831 --> 00:11:17,082
You think so, huh?

189
00:11:19,292 --> 00:11:20,377
Yeah.

190
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:26,258
Look at these two.
There's absolutely no commitment.

191
00:11:26,758 --> 00:11:27,843
We're getting close.

192
00:11:27,968 --> 00:11:30,303
Now he just needs to move
that arm, and we're all good.

193
00:11:31,721 --> 00:11:34,349
It's guarding behavior.
Jane told me to watch for it.

194
00:11:34,474 --> 00:11:36,560
He moves that arm, it means
he's comfortable with Abbott.

195
00:11:38,228 --> 00:11:41,231
- Maybe he's not gonna do it.
- No, he's gonna do it. Watch.

196
00:11:43,942 --> 00:11:45,986
- There you go.
- Cool.

197
00:11:46,319 --> 00:11:48,905
I'll let Jane know
the mark has been roped.

198
00:11:56,371 --> 00:11:57,914
- Okay.
- All right.

199
00:12:04,296 --> 00:12:05,839
- All right, let me finish.
- Yeah, all right.

200
00:12:05,964 --> 00:12:07,924
Okay, so...
so, I've got him.

201
00:12:08,049 --> 00:12:11,261
I got him up against the ropes,
and I... bam! Pop him in the liver.

202
00:12:11,386 --> 00:12:14,097
The sucker folds in half
like a deck chair.

203
00:12:16,975 --> 00:12:19,394
- It was perfect.
- There's nothing like a liver shot

204
00:12:19,519 --> 00:12:21,396
to make a grown man
call for his mama.

205
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:22,981
- Ain't that the truth?
- Right?

206
00:12:23,106 --> 00:12:24,858
- You want another shot?
- Hey, line 'em up, man.

207
00:12:24,983 --> 00:12:26,234
Two.

208
00:12:29,738 --> 00:12:31,448
That was perfect.

209
00:12:33,158 --> 00:12:34,659
Absolutely.

210
00:12:39,539 --> 00:12:41,875
Perfect. Oh, man.

211
00:12:47,881 --> 00:12:49,549
That is perfect.

212
00:12:49,799 --> 00:12:51,718
- Oh, man.
- Look at these guys!

213
00:12:52,636 --> 00:12:54,179
You have an annoying laugh.

214
00:12:56,348 --> 00:12:57,557
What's that?

215
00:12:57,682 --> 00:12:59,601
A loud, annoying laugh.
You made me miss my shot.

216
00:12:59,726 --> 00:13:02,062
Hey, man, you miss, you lose.
Live with it.

217
00:13:02,187 --> 00:13:05,023
- Don't touch the money!
- Hey, brother, take my advice.

218
00:13:05,148 --> 00:13:07,025
Get out of my face
and go back to your game.

219
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:08,652
I'm not your brother.
And what are you looking at?

220
00:13:09,736 --> 00:13:11,696
Whoa! Hey, hey!

221
00:13:14,032 --> 00:13:16,326
- What's wrong with you, man?
- Yeah man.

222
00:13:18,411 --> 00:13:19,704
He's got a gun!

223
00:13:22,040 --> 00:13:23,583
Yeah, you better run.

224
00:13:26,753 --> 00:13:29,256
- That dude is crazy!
- Yeah, you got that right.

225
00:13:29,547 --> 00:13:31,675
My laugh is not annoying.

226
00:13:32,300 --> 00:13:34,594
Well, I guess we have to find
another place to drink.

227
00:13:35,804 --> 00:13:36,930
This yours?

228
00:13:37,764 --> 00:13:39,224
Yeah. Why?
You need a lift?

229
00:13:40,976 --> 00:13:42,727
- Yeah.
- All right. Come on.

230
00:13:43,478 --> 00:13:44,646
Thanks.

231
00:14:04,791 --> 00:14:06,334
Whew! We're here.

232
00:14:07,419 --> 00:14:10,922
- This is our new drinking place?
- Yeah. It's my boss's house.

233
00:14:12,007 --> 00:14:13,216
Swag.

234
00:14:20,015 --> 00:14:22,851
If you don't mind me asking
what line of business you in?

235
00:14:23,268 --> 00:14:24,602
Whatever needs doing.

236
00:14:27,355 --> 00:14:30,483
- Hey!
- Dennis, you wicked man.

237
00:14:30,608 --> 00:14:34,529
Where have you been?
And who is this handsome brute?

238
00:14:34,779 --> 00:14:36,698
Teresa, this is my friend Aaron.

239
00:14:37,532 --> 00:14:40,785
Fabulous to meet you, Aaron.
Any friend of Dennis. Come on in.

240
00:14:56,115 --> 00:15:00,369
Dennis, you old rascal.
I am so glad you came.

241
00:15:03,039 --> 00:15:04,624
And who do we have here?

242
00:15:05,208 --> 00:15:08,377
- This is my friend Aaron.
- Aaron, pleasure to meet you.

243
00:15:09,253 --> 00:15:12,131
- Pisces?
- No. Sagittarius.

244
00:15:12,673 --> 00:15:14,967
Close, but no cigar.
I like you anyway.

245
00:15:15,635 --> 00:15:19,263
And you can relax
make yourself at home, grab a drink.

246
00:15:19,639 --> 00:15:23,601
Oh, isn't he fabulous?
Such a mean face.

247
00:15:24,227 --> 00:15:26,812
- Yeah. There it is.
- Down, girl.

248
00:15:28,564 --> 00:15:31,692
You're doing very well.
Just a little, uh... a little big.

249
00:15:31,817 --> 00:15:34,362
- Big? Really?
- Yeah, try a little less Joan Crawford

250
00:15:34,487 --> 00:15:36,697
- And a little more Barbara Stanwyck.
- How do you know what that means?

251
00:15:36,822 --> 00:15:39,325
Look, I can't walk,
and you're giving me acting tips?

252
00:15:40,076 --> 00:15:43,538
- Just stay upright. Keep smiling.
- I can't breathe in this dress.

253
00:15:44,497 --> 00:15:45,790
Can you pass the rice, please?

254
00:15:47,416 --> 00:15:48,709
Where did the house
come from?

255
00:15:48,834 --> 00:15:51,587
It's a drug forfeiture,
on loan from the D.E.A.

256
00:15:52,255 --> 00:15:55,258
so, what's the story
with Jane and Lisbon?

257
00:15:56,092 --> 00:15:59,512
She was working for the California
bureau of investigation in Sacramento

258
00:15:59,637 --> 00:16:03,015
- and then his wife and child were...
- No, I mean, are they in a relationship?

259
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:04,809
- A couple?
- No, no.

260
00:16:04,934 --> 00:16:06,185
I used to think so,
but they're not.

261
00:16:07,061 --> 00:16:08,229
I don't think.

262
00:16:10,022 --> 00:16:11,607
- How's it going?
- It's going good.

263
00:16:11,732 --> 00:16:14,569
- You got a nice right cross.
- Thanks.

264
00:16:14,694 --> 00:16:16,779
We just ate. There's some
vindaloo left, I think, if you want.

265
00:16:16,904 --> 00:16:18,072
No, I'm good.

266
00:16:18,197 --> 00:16:20,491
So, Abbott roped the mark,
got him to the party.

267
00:16:20,616 --> 00:16:22,618
He's met the inside man...
Jane, that is.

268
00:16:22,743 --> 00:16:25,454
Now we're just waiting on the face
to lure the mark to the bedroom.

269
00:16:25,621 --> 00:16:27,707
- Has he been talking like this all night?
- Yeah.

270
00:16:28,374 --> 00:16:29,584
Ah, here we go.

271
00:16:35,506 --> 00:16:36,632
Hey, now.

272
00:16:38,009 --> 00:16:39,343
"Hey, now," yourself.

273
00:16:40,678 --> 00:16:43,180
- What's your name, sweetheart?
- Kim.

274
00:16:44,223 --> 00:16:45,266
Aaron.

275
00:16:46,392 --> 00:16:48,477
You are smoking hot.

276
00:16:49,687 --> 00:16:52,398
You're like a damn fire hazard
walking around here.

277
00:16:52,523 --> 00:16:54,692
- Oh, hush.
- No, for real.

278
00:16:58,904 --> 00:17:00,448
The eagle has landed.

279
00:17:07,455 --> 00:17:09,206
We only just met.

280
00:17:09,457 --> 00:17:11,876
Oh, I feel like we've known
each other so long.

281
00:17:13,294 --> 00:17:15,504
Perhaps we hooked up
in a previous life.

282
00:17:15,963 --> 00:17:21,552
Oh... You mean like you were
Napoleon and I was Josephine?

283
00:17:21,927 --> 00:17:25,473
I was thinking more like Mickey Rourke
and Kim Basinger, you know what I'm saying?

284
00:17:26,724 --> 00:17:30,436
You are a very naughty boy.

285
00:18:03,302 --> 00:18:06,806
Whoa, whoa.
Slow down there, cowboy.

286
00:18:08,349 --> 00:18:10,393
What's the matter, baby?
Come here.

287
00:18:10,518 --> 00:18:12,186
When I said,
"make yourselves at home"

288
00:18:12,311 --> 00:18:15,731
that's not exactly what I meant.
That's... that's my bed.

289
00:18:16,065 --> 00:18:19,235
I'm sorry, man.
You see a chance like this, you take it.

290
00:18:19,568 --> 00:18:23,698
Kim, really?
You have no self-control. Out.

291
00:18:23,823 --> 00:18:25,241
Sorry, boss.

292
00:18:27,034 --> 00:18:28,703
Anything that moves.

293
00:18:30,496 --> 00:18:31,789
What the hell?

294
00:18:32,915 --> 00:18:34,250
Is that a Van Gogh?

295
00:18:35,042 --> 00:18:37,336
I'd never have pegged you
as an art lover.

296
00:18:37,461 --> 00:18:38,713
It's a fake.

297
00:18:41,757 --> 00:18:44,260
- All of it?
- Yeah, afraid so.

298
00:18:44,635 --> 00:18:46,387
I couldn't afford them
if they were real.

299
00:18:48,055 --> 00:18:50,683
What is it that you do?
I mean for money?

300
00:18:51,308 --> 00:18:53,644
- Software.
- Software?

301
00:18:53,936 --> 00:18:55,730
Haven't seen a computer
in your whole house.

302
00:18:58,190 --> 00:19:00,359
Never take your work home with you.

303
00:19:00,985 --> 00:19:02,403
Key to a happy life.

304
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:06,866
Let's go downstairs,
have some fun.

305
00:19:12,580 --> 00:19:15,875
Hey. It's Pulaski.
I think I've found something.

306
00:19:16,542 --> 00:19:19,336
Nope. You're gonna
want to see this yourself.

307
00:19:23,883 --> 00:19:27,136
Thanks for the party.
I had a great time.

308
00:19:27,762 --> 00:19:29,096
You get her home safe.

309
00:19:29,805 --> 00:19:31,223
I can take her home.

310
00:19:33,434 --> 00:19:35,227
Maybe in the next life.

311
00:19:37,021 --> 00:19:38,147
Right.

312
00:19:40,816 --> 00:19:42,943
Looks like your ride ditched you.

313
00:19:44,153 --> 00:19:47,031
- Can I call you a car?
- No, I'm good.

314
00:19:48,365 --> 00:19:50,785
- Thanks for the party.
- My pleasure.

315
00:20:02,046 --> 00:20:04,882
Patrick? Are you up there?

316
00:20:05,549 --> 00:20:06,967
Everybody's gone.

317
00:20:18,187 --> 00:20:20,689
"I'm sorry. The party's over."

318
00:20:21,690 --> 00:20:23,067
"Where did you get this painting?"

319
00:20:23,359 --> 00:20:25,444
"My boyfriend will know.
He's on his way up."

320
00:20:26,821 --> 00:20:28,531
All right, fellas,
what's the situation?

321
00:20:29,031 --> 00:20:32,660
Well, Pulaski left a sliding door open
before he left and this guy came through it.

322
00:20:33,911 --> 00:20:35,704
- We got a name?
- I'm working on it.

323
00:20:40,376 --> 00:20:43,295
Oh, I keep finding strangers
in my bedroom.

324
00:20:43,420 --> 00:20:44,839
Guess it was a good party.

325
00:20:45,422 --> 00:20:47,633
- You all right, Teresa?
- I am now that you're here.

326
00:20:47,758 --> 00:20:49,760
- He wants to know about the painting.
- It's a fake.

327
00:20:49,885 --> 00:20:54,223
No, it isn't. It's the real Mccoy.
It was stolen two years ago.

328
00:20:54,932 --> 00:20:58,060
- Really?
- Yeah. So...

329
00:20:59,812 --> 00:21:01,021
What's the deal?

330
00:21:02,106 --> 00:21:05,484
Uh... I don't know that we've met.
I'm... I'm Patrick.

331
00:21:07,695 --> 00:21:09,655
- And this is Teresa.
- Hi.

332
00:21:12,283 --> 00:21:13,409
MacKaye.

333
00:21:15,077 --> 00:21:17,997
I'll cross-reference the name
"MacKaye" with the picture.

334
00:21:20,708 --> 00:21:23,794
Edwin MacKaye, a Dallas
resident, business owner.

335
00:21:23,919 --> 00:21:26,755
He owns some local restaurants
Fast-food joints, that kind of thing.

336
00:21:27,006 --> 00:21:29,049
Cash business... that's good
for money laundering.

337
00:21:29,174 --> 00:21:32,720
- Criminal record?
- No. He's clean. Ex-military.

338
00:21:34,013 --> 00:21:36,765
Oh, and look...
a minor in art history.

339
00:21:37,016 --> 00:21:39,935
Oh, that's our boy.
If Jane's theory is right

340
00:21:40,144 --> 00:21:41,604
then he should be
the leader of the gang

341
00:21:41,729 --> 00:21:43,689
which means he's the one
that killed Hennigan.

342
00:21:44,273 --> 00:21:47,234
Okay, cut the cute stuff.
You stole these.

343
00:21:47,359 --> 00:21:50,571
It's what you do.
I mean, these are all stolen.

344
00:21:51,822 --> 00:21:54,158
How do you know so much about it?
Are you a cop?

345
00:21:54,700 --> 00:21:56,201
Oh, don't you get it?

346
00:21:56,952 --> 00:22:00,664
- This gentleman's one of us.
- He doesn't look like one of us.

347
00:22:01,415 --> 00:22:03,959
No. He's a heavy, a shooter.

348
00:22:04,668 --> 00:22:06,086
I'm direct.

349
00:22:06,795 --> 00:22:10,424
And you're one of those grifter types, huh?
You, uh, prefer tricks?

350
00:22:11,383 --> 00:22:12,593
You could say that.

351
00:22:12,718 --> 00:22:15,346
Eh. Never saw a trick
that could beat a bullet.

352
00:22:15,804 --> 00:22:18,307
Well, it's always nice to get tips
from the competition.

353
00:22:18,432 --> 00:22:20,184
Oh, I-I don't have competition.

354
00:22:21,101 --> 00:22:22,937
See, Texas is my territory.

355
00:22:23,812 --> 00:22:26,941
You've got to go someplace else.
I hear Miami's hot.

356
00:22:27,232 --> 00:22:31,236
Are you saying this town
ain't big enough for the two of us?

357
00:22:31,362 --> 00:22:32,613
Yeah.

358
00:22:35,324 --> 00:22:36,825
I'm not playing with you.

359
00:22:36,951 --> 00:22:39,578
Oh, I get it.
But I think you're a businessman.

360
00:22:39,787 --> 00:22:41,413
Now, you can try to run us
out of town

361
00:22:41,538 --> 00:22:45,084
or maybe you can stop and think
about what brought us to town.

362
00:22:45,209 --> 00:22:47,044
- You got a job planned?
- A big one.

363
00:22:47,586 --> 00:22:50,589
- The Vermeer at the DMA?
- Lousy escape routes.

364
00:22:52,341 --> 00:22:54,093
- The hirst sculpture?
- Too heavy.

365
00:22:57,846 --> 00:23:01,183
- Violets.
- Give the man a cigar. He's a sharp one.

366
00:23:01,767 --> 00:23:04,520
Well, I guess you've got
three options.

367
00:23:04,812 --> 00:23:08,857
You can cut me in on the job
you can get out of town tonight

368
00:23:09,566 --> 00:23:13,070
or I just hurt the two of you badly.
What do you think?

369
00:23:13,195 --> 00:23:14,363
You know, you should have
been a salesman.

370
00:23:14,488 --> 00:23:17,825
What do you say darling?
Are we gonna cut this thug in on our plan?

371
00:23:17,950 --> 00:23:20,285
It's better than him
hurting us badly.

372
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:26,500
Welcome to the team.

373
00:24:19,993 --> 00:24:22,579
- This is Pike.
- "Hey. It's Lisbon."

374
00:24:22,704 --> 00:24:24,665
I'm just checking in
before I go to bed.

375
00:24:24,790 --> 00:24:27,584
"Okay, well, we, uh...
we shut down the inside cameras"

376
00:24:27,709 --> 00:24:30,003
so you don't have to worry about
any more prying eyes.

377
00:24:30,128 --> 00:24:32,339
And, just so you know,
there is someone outside the house.

378
00:24:32,464 --> 00:24:34,424
We're guessing it's another one
of MacKaye's crew.

379
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:36,551
Well, that's gonna
make me sleep better.

380
00:24:36,677 --> 00:24:38,512
"No, we've got eyes on him.
Don't worry."

381
00:24:38,637 --> 00:24:41,098
You're safe.
I wouldn't lie to you.

382
00:24:41,556 --> 00:24:43,392
That's a nice
change of pace.

383
00:24:43,642 --> 00:24:46,228
I feel like everything
I've done today is a lie.

384
00:24:46,520 --> 00:24:49,481
Well, you were, uh...
you were real good.

385
00:24:49,898 --> 00:24:51,400
"I learned from the best."

386
00:24:52,818 --> 00:24:56,571
I'm starving, and there's nothing
to eat in this house.

387
00:24:57,030 --> 00:24:58,407
Well, that's a shame.

388
00:24:59,116 --> 00:25:00,951
There's a pretty good diner
just down the street from you...

389
00:25:01,076 --> 00:25:02,869
Biscuits and gravy,
if that's your thing.

390
00:25:03,161 --> 00:25:06,081
- Not really.
- All right, well, what about pancakes?

391
00:25:06,540 --> 00:25:08,583
I could go for some pancakes.

392
00:25:09,042 --> 00:25:12,796
They have about six kinds...
chocolate chip, banana

393
00:25:12,921 --> 00:25:15,799
- "All that good stuff."
- Now you're just trying to torture me.

394
00:25:15,966 --> 00:25:19,720
Maybe. Just a little bit.
I could have some sent over to you.

395
00:25:19,928 --> 00:25:22,264
You said there's a guy
watching the house, remember?

396
00:25:22,597 --> 00:25:25,726
Yeah, you're right. I won't tease you
anymore. I won't even mention the waffles.

397
00:25:26,351 --> 00:25:29,771
I'm gonna go downstairs and see
if there's some canapés left from the party.

398
00:25:29,938 --> 00:25:32,274
All right, well, I don't know
what a canapé is. But bon appétit.

399
00:25:33,358 --> 00:25:35,360
- Good night, Lisbon.
- Good night.

400
00:25:44,369 --> 00:25:45,412
Fischer.

401
00:25:46,121 --> 00:25:48,165
Good job on getting Pulaski
to the bedroom.

402
00:25:49,041 --> 00:25:51,209
Candy from a baby.
How did we do?

403
00:25:51,418 --> 00:25:54,463
This guy paid Jane and Lisbon a visit
after the party was over... Edwin MacKaye.

404
00:25:54,588 --> 00:25:55,756
Looks like he's the one
who killed Hennigan.

405
00:25:55,881 --> 00:25:56,923
So what's our next move?

406
00:25:57,049 --> 00:25:59,009
Jane's plan's working so far,
so we stick with it.

407
00:25:59,176 --> 00:26:01,428
Yeah. I want you to handle the takedown.
It's gonna be dangerous.

408
00:26:01,553 --> 00:26:02,804
- You got it.
- All right.

409
00:26:03,472 --> 00:26:05,307
Fischer, I want you
to handle the streets.

410
00:26:06,850 --> 00:26:08,769
Jane gave us a list of things
that he needs.

411
00:26:11,354 --> 00:26:12,898
A marching band?

412
00:26:13,023 --> 00:26:16,777
- Yeah. A marching band.
- Yes, sir.

413
00:26:24,034 --> 00:26:26,953
Well, you just love
popping up places, don't you?

414
00:26:27,079 --> 00:26:30,373
- Let's go.
- And good morning to you, too.

415
00:26:32,250 --> 00:26:33,376
Shotgun.

416
00:26:47,974 --> 00:26:51,103
"Manet painted it as
a gift for his brother's wife."

417
00:26:52,145 --> 00:26:53,522
His forbidden love.

418
00:26:54,022 --> 00:26:56,108
He couldn't express
his love to her in words

419
00:26:56,233 --> 00:26:59,986
so he did it a different way...
with a painting.

420
00:27:00,112 --> 00:27:02,906
It's worth maybe $100 million
if it ever went to auction.

421
00:27:03,490 --> 00:27:05,867
- You have someone who wants it?
- We've got a customer lined up.

422
00:27:05,992 --> 00:27:07,536
- How much?
- $5 million.

423
00:27:07,661 --> 00:27:08,870
50/50 split.

424
00:27:11,998 --> 00:27:13,041
Fair enough.

425
00:27:14,459 --> 00:27:17,295
Now, you've probably got some
cute, little plan to get us in and out

426
00:27:17,420 --> 00:27:19,005
no fuss, no muss, huh?

427
00:27:19,548 --> 00:27:22,384
Well, I do like to keep
my muss to a minimum.

428
00:27:22,509 --> 00:27:23,635
I don't do cute.

429
00:27:24,344 --> 00:27:26,596
Tricks, cleverness...
it's a waste of time.

430
00:27:26,888 --> 00:27:29,683
- You'd be surprised.
- Ah, fast and simple.

431
00:27:29,808 --> 00:27:31,101
"That's the way to do things."

432
00:27:31,518 --> 00:27:33,270
"And you're the one
behind the wheel."

433
00:27:33,603 --> 00:27:36,523
This is the part I don't understand.
We're not gonna rob the museum.

434
00:27:36,690 --> 00:27:38,233
- No.
- Why not?

435
00:27:38,358 --> 00:27:40,152
I mean, he robs the painting
from the museum

436
00:27:40,277 --> 00:27:42,654
we catch him red-handed,
case closed, we all go home.

437
00:27:42,779 --> 00:27:45,866
But then we wouldn't be able to prove
that he murdered John Hennigan.

438
00:27:46,741 --> 00:27:50,120
Besides, Jane said that MacKaye
doesn't want to rob the museum.

439
00:27:50,245 --> 00:27:51,788
Then why is he at the museum
with Jane?

440
00:27:51,913 --> 00:27:53,915
'Cause he wants to get Jane
and Lisbon out of the house.

441
00:27:54,040 --> 00:27:56,543
That's okay, 'cause we know
what they're gonna do next.

442
00:29:09,199 --> 00:29:10,408
Well, damn.

443
00:29:47,529 --> 00:29:50,407
- So, they hid all the paintings.
- Yep.

444
00:29:51,074 --> 00:29:52,325
Thinks he's smart.

445
00:29:52,826 --> 00:29:54,411
Thinks he's pulling one
over on me.

446
00:29:55,078 --> 00:29:56,454
So, what do we do?

447
00:29:56,579 --> 00:30:00,000
We hit them... the old-fashioned
way, hard and fast.

448
00:30:00,208 --> 00:30:03,712
I figure four minutes.
We get in, we open the safe, we get out.

449
00:30:04,087 --> 00:30:06,923
And what if this Patrick guy stalls
in giving us the combinations?

450
00:30:08,591 --> 00:30:10,760
He acts like he doesn't care
about anything

451
00:30:11,344 --> 00:30:13,346
but I see the way
he looks at his girlfriend.

452
00:30:13,471 --> 00:30:15,223
See, that...
that's the soft spot.

453
00:30:15,932 --> 00:30:19,227
He gives us any trouble
I shoot the brunette in the head.

454
00:30:32,905 --> 00:30:35,783
We need to keep at least one of them alive
until we get the safe open.

455
00:30:35,992 --> 00:30:40,246
After that, if one of them gets in the way
it's open season.

456
00:31:03,311 --> 00:31:06,731
- Up, now!
- Whoa, sorry. Didn't hear the doorbell.

457
00:31:06,856 --> 00:31:09,108
No talking.
To the bedroom... now.

458
00:31:09,233 --> 00:31:11,736
Stay with her.
If I give the word, kill her.

459
00:31:11,903 --> 00:31:14,655
- Let's go!
- I'll be back in two shakes, babe.

460
00:31:16,407 --> 00:31:18,993
- Move.
- Oh, so demanding.

461
00:31:21,996 --> 00:31:25,124
Would you like a drink?
We have some champagne right here.

462
00:31:29,921 --> 00:31:32,382
Don't say a word.
Drop the gun now.

463
00:31:44,685 --> 00:31:45,728
Open the safe.

464
00:31:46,354 --> 00:31:49,607
Oh, you know about the safe.
That's a depressing development.

465
00:31:54,862 --> 00:31:59,409
- Faster!
- Yeah, slow and steady opens the safe.

466
00:32:08,376 --> 00:32:09,460
Voilà.

467
00:32:17,176 --> 00:32:18,219
Here.

468
00:32:27,061 --> 00:32:28,771
- Hold on.
- What?

469
00:32:30,940 --> 00:32:32,650
I don't like the look on his face.

470
00:32:40,450 --> 00:32:42,160
Tracking device, yeah?

471
00:32:43,744 --> 00:32:44,996
You think I'm stupid?

472
00:32:45,121 --> 00:32:47,373
Ask me that question again
when you don't have a gun.

473
00:32:54,672 --> 00:32:55,715
Okay.

474
00:32:56,549 --> 00:32:58,593
You stay up here for a full minute
after we're gone.

475
00:32:58,718 --> 00:33:00,803
- You understand?
- Yep.

476
00:33:06,642 --> 00:33:07,977
One, Mississippi...

477
00:33:13,065 --> 00:33:14,734
Shouldn't have come back, fellas.

478
00:33:14,859 --> 00:33:17,153
Now, why don't you put the guns down
so we can discuss it?

479
00:33:26,621 --> 00:33:27,788
Bastard.

480
00:33:43,095 --> 00:33:44,347
He's in the back!

481
00:34:00,279 --> 00:34:01,906
Hey! What you're doin'?

482
00:35:11,392 --> 00:35:12,560
Hey!

483
00:35:18,399 --> 00:35:19,609
Okay.

484
00:35:20,860 --> 00:35:23,404
2235 Fritz street, off Singleton.

485
00:35:23,988 --> 00:35:25,281
Yes, sir.

486
00:35:41,839 --> 00:35:44,133
- Keep the change.
- Thanks. Have a nice day.

487
00:36:19,835 --> 00:36:21,754
FBI! Put your hands
on your head!

488
00:36:21,879 --> 00:36:22,963
Do it now!

489
00:36:32,640 --> 00:36:33,724
All right.

490
00:36:34,850 --> 00:36:37,520
Let's be very clear
about something.

491
00:36:37,853 --> 00:36:40,022
We don't need you
to say anything.

492
00:36:40,690 --> 00:36:42,983
We already have the rest
of your crew in custody.

493
00:36:43,109 --> 00:36:45,653
We have FBI witnesses
for armed robbery

494
00:36:46,112 --> 00:36:48,114
assault, kidnapping.

495
00:36:49,740 --> 00:36:53,202
We have enough stolen art
to start a museum.

496
00:36:53,327 --> 00:36:54,453
And, best of all

497
00:36:55,413 --> 00:36:58,416
we have the paintings
that prove you killed John Hennigan.

498
00:36:59,250 --> 00:37:01,502
- Lawyer.
- Lawyer.

499
00:37:03,963 --> 00:37:05,756
Well, um, that is your right.

500
00:37:06,048 --> 00:37:08,884
Oh, what song was the marching
band playing, by the way?

501
00:37:10,803 --> 00:37:12,680
The marching band that,
blocked off the alleyway.

502
00:37:12,805 --> 00:37:15,558
- What song were they playing?
- What are you talking about?

503
00:37:15,725 --> 00:37:18,060
Well, I requested
"the yellow rose of Texas."

504
00:37:18,185 --> 00:37:19,812
Were they playing
"the yellow rose of Texas"?

505
00:37:29,405 --> 00:37:32,158
The garbage truck, the marching
band... you did all that?

506
00:37:37,580 --> 00:37:39,749
You steered me to the cab, huh?

507
00:37:40,082 --> 00:37:41,417
The illusion of control.

508
00:37:41,834 --> 00:37:44,795
It made you feel safe enough to go
where you kept the paintings.

509
00:37:45,629 --> 00:37:47,631
Now, unless you have
any further questions

510
00:37:47,757 --> 00:37:50,676
about how I played you
like a big, old bassoon

511
00:37:51,093 --> 00:37:54,346
I have someplace...
Fabulous to go.

512
00:37:58,350 --> 00:37:59,560
Au revoir.

513
00:38:08,444 --> 00:38:09,612
What's this?

514
00:38:09,737 --> 00:38:12,823
It's case-closed pizza. It's an old
tradition. Thought I'd bring it back.

515
00:38:14,158 --> 00:38:16,202
- Next time, it's on me.
- Deal.

516
00:38:19,455 --> 00:38:23,000
- How is it?
- It's all right.

517
00:38:23,125 --> 00:38:25,961
You probably like that
Chicago-style stuff, right?

518
00:38:26,629 --> 00:38:27,880
Pizza's pizza.

519
00:38:29,507 --> 00:38:31,050
I'm not really
in the mood for pizza.

520
00:38:31,509 --> 00:38:35,012
You know what sounds good?
Those pancakes we were talking about before.

521
00:38:35,137 --> 00:38:37,181
- Those did sound good.
- Do you want to go?

522
00:38:37,306 --> 00:38:38,933
- Right now?
- Yeah.

523
00:38:39,558 --> 00:38:41,852
Dinner is the best time
for breakfast.

524
00:38:42,812 --> 00:38:44,063
What do you say?

525
00:38:46,232 --> 00:38:48,359
That's really sweet, but...

526
00:38:49,235 --> 00:38:53,072
look, if you don't want to go, just say so.
We're cool. It's good.

527
00:38:53,197 --> 00:38:55,699
But I like you and I think you're a hell
of a good-looking woman

528
00:38:55,825 --> 00:38:57,451
and I'd love
to get to know you better.

529
00:39:00,704 --> 00:39:02,289
- Let's do it.
- Let's go.

530
00:39:02,623 --> 00:39:03,666
All right.

531
00:39:11,590 --> 00:39:13,759
Excuse me.
I was told to come here.

532
00:39:13,884 --> 00:39:15,386
Oh, yes. Please, come in.

533
00:39:17,596 --> 00:39:19,974
Is it true you got the man
who killed john?

534
00:39:20,099 --> 00:39:22,518
Yes, I did, and I also got this.

535
00:39:34,488 --> 00:39:37,616
Don't... don't they need it
for the trial?

536
00:39:37,741 --> 00:39:39,201
Oh, no. Don't worry about that.

537
00:39:44,248 --> 00:39:45,749
It's true what you said.

538
00:39:46,625 --> 00:39:47,877
He's...

539
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:50,087
He's still here.

540
00:40:00,222 --> 00:40:02,057
Okay, so, where are we going?

541
00:40:05,644 --> 00:40:07,897
Um... well... uh, y...

542
00:40:08,022 --> 00:40:10,316
Oh, no, no. You... you kids
go ahead and have fun.

543
00:40:12,026 --> 00:40:13,777
- Good night, Jane.
- Good night.

544
00:40:13,903 --> 00:40:14,987
Okay.

545
00:40:15,613 --> 00:40:16,739
Have fun.


